// Design 174 — DF-146l trigger site 3 (NEW, found by this sweep). A bare `None`
// passed as a GENERIC CALL ARGUMENT ICEs instead of producing the clean
// "cannot infer type argument" error the underdetermined case deserves:
//
//   internal compiler error: None literal has no type information
//
// `idn(None)` is genuinely underdetermined — nothing says what `T` is — so the
// correct outcome is a clean anchored error naming `T`, exactly as
// `idn<Int?>(None)` (which works) is the way to say it. What must not happen is
// an ICE. The EXPECT below therefore pins the ERROR, not a successful run.
//
// FIXED by design 176 unit 1: a bare `None` no longer BINDS a type parameter
// during inference. It has no type of its own, so it constrains nothing — which
// leaves this call underdetermined and reported as such, and (DF-174f) leaves a
// later argument free to fix the parameter instead.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: cannot infer type argument

func idn<T>(x: T) -> T { x }

func main() {
    let r = idn(None)
    print("unreachable")
}
